import{SRC,range,bits,f,consensusCheck,flood,hiddenChain,fullInformation,twoPhase,threePhase,asynchronousAudit,primitiveConsensus,panel,grid,timeline,player}from'./ch05.shared.js';
export default{collection:'dist',chapter:5,title:'Consensus and its impossibility',summary:String.raw`Consensus guarantees depend on timing, failures and progress assumptions. We execute finite protocols, inspect their invariants, and separate those experiments from impossibility proofs over all admissible executions. Randomness, stronger shared objects and eventual synchrony change the model rather than contradicting FLP.`,items:[
{
num:'5.1',kind:'Definition',name:'The consensus problem',
statement:String.raw`Each process proposes a value and may irreversibly decide. Proposal validity requires every decision to equal some proposed value. Uniform agreement requires all decisions to agree, including decisions by processes that later fail. Termination requires every correct process eventually to decide in every admissible execution. Correct means never crashing in that execution; admissibility specifies timing, delivery and scheduling assumptions.`,
idea:String.raw`Agreement and validity concern finite events: conflicting decisions or an unproposed decision supply witnesses. Eventual termination quantifies over entire infinite executions, so a pending process in a finite prefix does not by itself violate termination. A known finite round deadline can instead be checked at its deadline.`,
caption:String.raw`Choose an authored candidate and a delivery prefix. Each row shows input, received values and decision; the live checker distinguishes witnessed safety failures from unresolved progress. The wait-for-all candidate is safe but blocks if a sender crashes before broadcasting.`,
deps:['dist-1.3'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture4 slides13–17; Herlihy1991 §3 consensus specification'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['consensus','safety','liveness'],scope:String.raw`Three deterministic processes, immutable binary inputs, reliable initial broadcasts from noncrashed senders, at most one initial crash. The display is a finite delivery prefix. Early quorum/min is an intentionally unsafe candidate; own-input is another counterexample, not a consensus algorithm. Correct-process completion is measured only in the shown prefix.`,
viz(v){const mask=v.slider('proposal mask',0,7,3,{int:true}),mode=v.select('candidate',['wait for all','early quorum','own input'],'wait for all'),crash=v.select('initial crash',['none','P0','P1','P2'],'none'),seed=v.slider('delivery seed',1,100,1,{int:true}),step=v.slider('delivered messages',0,6,3,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'local evidence');let A,C;v.render(()=>{const cp=crash.value==='none'?-1:+crash.value.slice(1);A=asynchronousAudit(mask.value,mode.value==='early quorum'?2:3,seed.value,cp,step.value);if(mode.value==='own input')A.decisions=A.input.map((x,i)=>i===cp?null:x);C=consensusCheck(A.input,A.decisions,A.correct);const frame=A.frames.at(-1);grid(s,A.input.map((x,i)=>[x,...frame.inbox[i],A.decisions[i]===null?'?':A.decisions[i]]));});v.legend([['blue','0'],['gold','1'],['purple','? pending; columns: input / inbox / decision']]);v.readout(()=>`Uniform agreement ${C.agreement?'holds so far':'VIOLATED'}; proposal validity ${C.validity?'holds so far':'VIOLATED'}. Correct processes completed ${A.correct.filter(i=>A.decisions[i]!==null).length}/${A.correct.length}. Pending after this prefix is not a proof of nontermination. Queued broadcast messages ${A.pending.length}; shown ${Math.min(step.value,A.pending.length)}.`);}
},
{
num:'5.2',kind:'Theorem',name:'The two generals problem is unsolvable',
statement:String.raw`Two deterministic generals communicating over a link that can lose any messages cannot guarantee agreement and termination, together with weak validity: both start0 implies decision0; both start1 and every message delivered implies decision1. This holds even with synchronous rounds. The protocol must tolerate arbitrary losses, including silent infinite tails; it cannot assume a final acknowledgement is reliable.`,
idea:String.raw`Take an all-delivered execution and a finite round by which both generals decide. First discard all messages after that round, obtaining a finite delivered prefix without changing either decision. Then delete its last delivered message. The sender's local view is unchanged; termination and agreement force the same eventual decision at the receiver, even if its decision is later. Repeat until no messages are delivered. In this silent execution changing the unseen general's input leaves the other's view unchanged, contradicting the all0 validity condition. This argument concerns an assumed correct protocol, not the particular transcript drawn.`,
caption:String.raw`Remove delivered messages from the end of a full-information exchange. Exact local transcript strings show which sender retains the same view at the next deletion. The diagram illustrates the indistinguishability step; it does not invent a successful attack protocol.`,
deps:['dist-5.1','dist-1.7'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture4 slides3–12'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['indistinguishability','lossy channels'],scope:String.raw`The authored alternating transcript has both initial inputs1, sends on every prescribed round, and includes the entire sender transcript in each payload. Losses are legal. Its views can be compared exactly, but no decisions are assigned to this exchange. The theorem's validity and termination premises are stated separately.`,
viz(v){const rounds=v.slider('scheduled messages',1,6,4,{int:true}),keep=v.slider('delivered prefix',0,6,3,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'last delivered message deletion');let A,B,k,unchanged;v.render(()=>{k=Math.min(keep.value,rounds.value);A=fullInformation(rounds.value,k);B=fullInformation(rounds.value,Math.max(0,k-1));unchanged=A.views.map((x,i)=>x===B.views[i]);s.setView([-.5,rounds.value+.6],[-.5,1.7]);for(let p=0;p<2;p++){s.path([[0,p],[rounds.value,p]],{color:'muted'});s.text(p?'B':'A',-.25,p,{align:'center'});}A.messages.forEach(e=>s.path([[e.t-.6,e.p],[e.t,e.q]],{color:e.ok?'blue':'red',dash:!e.ok,width:e.t===k?3:1.5}));});v.legend([['blue','delivered; thick = next deletion'],['red','lost']]);v.readout(()=>`Delivered ${k}/${rounds.value}. Comparing prefixes ${k} and ${Math.max(0,k-1)}: A view ${unchanged[0]?'unchanged':'changes'}, B view ${unchanged[1]?'unchanged':'changes'}. Exact transcript lengths A ${A.views[0].length} versus ${B.views[0].length}; B ${A.views[1].length} versus ${B.views[1].length}. No attack decisions are fabricated.`);}
},
{
num:'5.3',kind:'Algorithm',name:'FloodSet: crash-tolerant consensus in f + 1 rounds',
statement:String.raw`In a complete synchronous message network with n processes and at most f<n crash-stop failures, FloodSet solves uniform consensus in f+1 rounds. Assume a totally ordered proposal domain. Initialize W_i to the singleton input; each round broadcast W_i and union all received sets. At the end of round f+1, every nonfailed process decides min W_i. During its crash round a process may send to an arbitrary subset; other messages arrive in that round.`,
idea:String.raw`Among f+1 rounds one contains no crash. At the end of a crash-free round every surviving process has the same union of all then-alive senders' sets. Subsequent unions preserve that equality among survivors. Every set entry originated at a proposal, proving proposal validity. Any process alive at the decision boundary has the same set, so later crashes do not undermine uniform agreement.`,
caption:String.raw`Execute a hidden-value crash chain or a crash-free run. Inspect the full message diagram and each process's current set; before the final clean round survivors can still disagree. The decision rule is evaluated only at its required deadline.`,
deps:['dist-5.1','dist-1.2'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture4 slides19–24'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['synchronous rounds','crash faults'],scope:String.raw`Authored n=f+2 with f between0 and3. In chain mode process p crashes during round p+1 after sending only to p+1. A crashed row retains its last local state but never decides. The visualization enumerates this specific adversarial family, not every crash pattern; the clean-round proof supplies the general guarantee.`,
viz(v){const faults=v.slider('failure bound f',0,3,2,{int:true}),mode=v.select('execution',['hidden chain','no crashes'],'hidden chain'),round=v.slider('round boundary',0,4,2,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'broadcasts and local sets'),g=panel(v,'sets at selected boundary');let A,t,n;v.render(()=>{n=faults.value+2;t=Math.min(round.value,faults.value+1);A=mode.value==='hidden chain'?hiddenChain(faults.value,t):flood(n,range(n).map(i=>i===0?0:1),t);timeline(s,A.frames,n);grid(g,A.sets);});v.legend([['blue','messages / value0'],['gold','value1'],['muted','small dot: crashed']]);v.readout(()=>`Round ${t}/${faults.value+1}; alive ${A.alive.filter(Boolean).length}/${n}. Survivor sets ${A.sets.filter((_,i)=>A.alive[i]).map(x=>'{'+x.join(',')+'}').join(' / ')}. ${t===faults.value+1?'Decisions: '+A.decisions.map(x=>x===null?'crashed':x).join(', '):'No decisions yet: the algorithm waits for its deadline.'}`);}
},
{
num:'5.4',kind:'Theorem',name:'f + 1 rounds are necessary',
statement:String.raw`For n≥f+2 and 0≤f≤n−2, no deterministic synchronous binary crash-consensus protocol can guarantee that every nonfaulty process decides by the end of round f in every execution with at most f crashes. Thus the general worst-case deadline f+1 is optimal. This is not a claim that every execution needs f+1 rounds or that early stopping is impossible.`,
idea:String.raw`Unanimous initial inputs force opposite decisions, so an adjacent-input argument yields an initial bivalent configuration. Indistinguishability between round extensions differing by one crashing sender preserves a bivalent continuation through the first f−1 rounds. A final critical round with enough surviving processes prevents a universal round-f decision. The proof quantifies over every proposed deterministic protocol; the concrete hidden chain below only demonstrates why early FloodSet can fail.`,
caption:String.raw`Move the boundary through a real hidden0 chain. A failed sender passes0 only to the next process. At round f one survivor still holds1 while another knows0; a crash-free extra broadcast resolves this particular execution.`,
deps:['dist-5.3','dist-1.7'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture6 slides3–15'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['round lower bound','bivalence'],scope:String.raw`Finite diagnostic for FloodSet with n=f+2, f1…4. Tentative min-set decisions before its deadline are explicitly hypothetical; they are not the valencies of arbitrary algorithms. The lower-bound theorem assumes deterministic algorithms, complete synchronous communication, and crash broadcasts that may reach arbitrary receiver subsets.`,
viz(v){const faults=v.slider('failure bound',1,4,2,{int:true}),round=v.slider('round boundary',0,5,2,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'where the hidden0 has reached');let A,t;v.render(()=>{t=Math.min(round.value,faults.value+1);A=hiddenChain(faults.value,t);s.setView([-.7,A.n-.3],[-.5,1.5]);for(let p=0;p<A.n;p++){const value=Math.min(...A.sets[p]);s.dot(p,value,{r:8,color:A.alive[p]?(value?'gold':'blue'):'muted'});s.text('P'+p,p,value+.25,{size:11,align:'center'});}s.hline(0,{color:'blue',dash:true});s.hline(1,{color:'gold',dash:true});});v.legend([['blue','knows0: tentative min0'],['gold','only knows1: tentative min1'],['muted','crashed']]);v.readout(()=>`Round ${t}; survivors' tentative minima ${A.decisions.filter(x=>x!==null).join(', ')}. ${new Set(A.decisions.filter(x=>x!==null)).size>1?'Deciding now would violate agreement in this FloodSet execution.':'This execution has matching survivor minima; that alone proves no universal deadline.'} Universal worst-case bound: f+1=${faults.value+1}, assuming n≥f+2.`);}
},
{
num:'5.5',kind:'Algorithm',name:'Two-phase commit',
statement:String.raw`Atomic commit requires uniform agreement, abort if any participant votes NO, commit in a failure-free unanimous-YES run, and an explicitly chosen termination condition. In two-phase commit the coordinator collects votes and records COMMIT only if every vote is YES; otherwise it records ABORT. It then broadcasts the decision. A prepared YES voter cannot safely decide just from a timeout after losing the coordinator: another participant may already have committed.`,
idea:String.raw`The single recorded coordinator decision gives agreement when delivered, and unanimity establishes commit validity. If a coordinator crashes partway through its decision broadcast, one prepared participant can know COMMIT while another knows only its own YES vote. Unilaterally aborting the latter can conflict with the former; waiting preserves safety but may block indefinitely without recovery information.`,
caption:String.raw`Change votes and crash the coordinator before recording or during its broadcast. Prepared U rows remain unresolved, while the failed coordinator's recorded decision stays visible. Compare a completed commit with the evidence available to an isolated participant.`,
deps:['dist-5.1'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture6 slides39–44'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['atomic commit','blocking'],scope:String.raw`Four participants including coordinator P0, synchronous vote collection and a second decision round, at most one coordinator crash, no recovery. U=prepared/uncertain, A=abort, C=commit. Initial NO voters may abort immediately. The two-round schedule is the lecture's basic protocol; real database logging/recovery is outside this model.`,
viz(v){const votes=v.slider('YES vote mask',0,15,15,{int:true}),crash=v.select('coordinator failure',['none','before decision','during broadcast'],'during broadcast'),recipients=v.slider('decision recipients',0,3,1,{int:true}),round=v.slider('phase boundary',0,2,2,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'votes, decision and unresolved participants');let A;v.render(()=>{recipients.el.style.display=crash.value==='during broadcast'?'':'none';A=twoPhase(votes.value,crash.value,recipients.value,round.value);timeline(s,A.frames,4);});v.legend([['green','C committed'],['red','A aborted'],['blue','U uncertain'],['muted','small dot: failed']]);v.readout(()=>`Votes ${A.input.join(', ')} (1=YES). Alive states ${A.states.filter((_,i)=>A.alive[i]).join(', ')}; unresolved ${A.states.filter((x,i)=>A.alive[i]&&x==='U').length}. Coordinator stored state ${A.states[0]}. No timeout-based decision is silently supplied; an unresolved finite prefix is consistent with permanent blocking if recovery never occurs.`);}
},
{
num:'5.6',kind:'Algorithm',name:'Three-phase commit',
statement:String.raw`With reliable synchronous rounds and crash-stop failures, a three-phase atomic-commit protocol can provide nonblocking termination. A coordinator gathers votes, broadcasts READY only after receiving every YES vote (otherwise ABORT), and then broadcasts COMMIT after completing the READY broadcast. Survivors recover using rotating coordinators and reported U/READY/ABORT/COMMIT states. The recovery rules require synchronous knowledge of which messages should already have arrived; they are not safe timeout rules for an arbitrarily partitioned asynchronous network.`,
idea:String.raw`The intermediate READY state prevents an alive uncertain process from coexisting with a commit decision. In the lecture protocol, ABORT excludes COMMIT and alive READY; COMMIT excludes ABORT and alive U. A recovery coordinator adopts a reported decision, aborts if every received state is U, and otherwise repeats READY then COMMIT. Each coordinator gets three rounds, so one surviving coordinator finishes within3n rounds.`,
caption:String.raw`Crash any one participant at any selected round, optionally interrupting its broadcast. The actual rotating-coordinator execution retains failed states and checks all three invariants at every boundary. Unlike a partition simulation, this model stays within the synchronous theorem's assumptions.`,
deps:['dist-5.5'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture6 slides45–52 (rotating recovery)'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['atomic commit','synchrony','recovery'],scope:String.raw`Four participants, one optional crash, twelve synchronous rounds; coordinators P0,P1,P2,P3 each get three rounds. Crash round0 means no crash. A broadcast interrupted by a crash reaches receiver IDs below the cutoff. All other sent messages arrive in the same round. Stable states are retained after crash; recovery never revives a process.`,
viz(v){const votes=v.slider('YES vote mask',0,15,15,{int:true}),pid=v.slider('crashing process',0,3,0,{int:true}),cr=v.slider('crash round (0 none)',0,12,2,{int:true}),cut=v.slider('receiver ID cutoff',0,4,1,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'three-round phases and rotating recovery');const S=player(v,()=>threePhase(votes.value,pid.value,cr.value,cut.value,12).frames,()=>[votes.value,pid.value,cr.value,cut.value].join('|'),['Initialize U; NO → ABORT','Round 1: collect votes/states','  Initial: all YES → READY','  Recovery: adopt decision;','    all U → ABORT; else READY','Round 2: broadcast state','  full READY send → COMMIT','Round 3: broadcast COMMIT','Rotate coordinator; repeat']);let A;v.render(()=>{pid.el.style.display=cut.el.style.display=cr.value?'':'none';const full=threePhase(votes.value,pid.value,cr.value,cut.value,12),F=full.frames[S.i];A={...full,states:F.states,alive:F.alive,frames:full.frames.slice(0,S.i+1)};timeline(s,A.frames,4);});v.legend([['green','C'],['red','A'],['gold','READY'],['blue','U']]);v.readout(()=>`Round ${S.i}/12. States ${A.states.join(', ')}; alive ${A.alive.map((x,i)=>x?'P'+i:null).filter(Boolean).join(', ')}. Invariants at all completed rounds: ${A.frames.every(x=>x.invariant!==false)?'PASS':'FAIL'}. Alive unresolved ${A.states.filter((x,i)=>A.alive[i]&&x!=='C'&&x!=='A').length}. This guarantee does not include network partitions or crash recovery.`);}
},
{
num:'5.7',kind:'Theorem',name:'FLP impossibility',
statement:String.raw`No deterministic protocol in a completely asynchronous reliable message-passing system with N≥2 processes can satisfy binary consensus safety and termination for every admissible run with at most one crash. An admissible run gives every correct process infinitely many steps and eventually delivers every message addressed to a correct process. Proposal validity, uniform agreement and immutable decisions imply the nontriviality and partial-correctness conditions used in the original proof.`,
idea:String.raw`An adjacent-input argument gives a bivalent initial configuration. For any enabled event e, the critical-event lemma finds a finite extension ending in e that remains bivalent, using commutativity of events at distinct processes. Repeating this while rotating processes and servicing old messages constructs an admissible infinite undecided run. The construction does not postpone one fixed correct-recipient message forever.`,
caption:String.raw`Audit two concrete finite protocol candidates: waiting for all initial broadcasts preserves agreement but can block on a crashed sender; deciding from an early quorum can disagree. Neither bounded trace nor matching observed decisions is labeled FLP valency.`,
deps:['dist-5.1','dist-1.7'],refs:['Fischer–Lynch–Paterson1985 Theorem1, Lemmas1–3, pp375–380'],links:{source:SRC.flp},tags:['FLP','asynchrony','bivalence'],scope:String.raw`Three-process finite delivery experiment, one initial crash optional, first-quorum/min decision rule. The theorem is about every deterministic protocol in the specified asynchronous model, not just these candidates. A finite pending prefix proves neither infinite nontermination nor the absence of a deciding extension.`,
viz(v){const mode=v.select('candidate',['wait for all','early quorum'],'early quorum'),mask=v.slider('input mask',0,7,3,{int:true}),crash=v.select('initial crash',['none','P0','P1','P2'],'none'),seed=v.slider('delivery seed',1,100,1,{int:true}),count=v.slider('delivery prefix',0,6,3,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'actual local inbox counts and decisions');let A,C;v.render(()=>{A=asynchronousAudit(mask.value,mode.value==='wait for all'?3:2,seed.value,crash.value==='none'?-1:+crash.value.slice(1),count.value);C=consensusCheck(A.input,A.decisions,A.correct);const F=A.frames.at(-1);grid(s,F.inbox.map((x,i)=>[...x,A.decisions[i]===null?'?':A.decisions[i]]));});v.legend([['blue','0'],['gold','1'],['purple','last entry: decision or ?']]);v.readout(()=>`Observed decisions ${A.decisions.map(x=>x===null?'pending':x).join(', ')}. Agreement ${C.agreement?'not refuted by this prefix':'VIOLATED'}; completed correct processes ${A.correct.filter(i=>A.decisions[i]!==null).length}/${A.correct.length}. No bivalent/univalent classification is inferred from this finite audit. A seeded schedule is not the general FLP adversary.`);}
},
{
num:'5.8',kind:'Theorem',name:'Consensus number hierarchy (Herlihy)',
statement:String.raw`The consensus number of a shared-object type X is the largest n for which atomic read/write registers and instances of X implement deterministic wait-free n-process consensus; it is infinite if unbounded. Registers have number1; test-and-set and FIFO queues have number2; compare-and-swap has infinite number. A type with number m cannot wait-free implement a higher-number type for more than m processes in this model.`,
idea:String.raw`A two-process test-and-set winner returns its published proposal and the loser reads the other proposal. Compare-and-swap atomically installs the first proposal in a sentinel-initialized cell, which all return. The upper bounds use critical bivalent states: commuting operations or overwriting states make opposite decisions indistinguishable to a remaining process. This is a shared-memory impossibility proof, not a direct invocation of the message-passing FLP model.`,
caption:String.raw`Execute publication and atomic operations in a seeded interleaving. The two-process TAS and arbitrary-process CAS protocols are genuine constructions. The naive three-process TAS extension and register-min candidate can fail; their failures illustrate candidates, not proofs of every upper bound.`,
deps:['dist-1.4'],see:['dist-5.7'],refs:['Herlihy1991 Definition1, Theorems1–5 and7, pp130–137'],links:{source:SRC.herlihy},tags:['wait freedom','consensus hierarchy'],scope:String.raw`2…4 processes and one invocation per process; publication and the primitive are atomic. A displayed TAS group also performs its subsequent read of the immutable published proposal with no intervening scheduled process; no enumeration of every atomic interleaving is claimed. TAS is guaranteed only with2 processes. Its intentionally invalid n>2 extension reads the next process's proposal on losing and may return an unpublished value. A ? decision is pending; a returned null is marked invalid explicitly. Finite interleavings do not prove generic impossibility.`,
viz(v){const n=v.slider('process count',2,4,2,{int:true}),primitive=v.select('primitive',['TAS','CAS','register min'],'TAS'),mask=v.slider('proposal mask',0,15,1,{int:true}),seed=v.slider('schedule seed',1,100,2,{int:true}),steps=v.slider('scheduled groups',0,12,8,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'publication / primitive / return');let A;v.render(()=>{A=primitiveConsensus(n.value,mask.value,primitive.value,seed.value,steps.value);grid(s,range(n.value).map(p=>[A.input[p],...A.trace.filter(e=>e.p===p).map(e=>e.label==='publish'?'pub':e.label==='register min'?'min':e.label==='return'?'ret':e.label)]),{colors:{pub:'blue',TAS:'gold',CAS:'green',min:'red',ret:'purple'}});});v.legend([['blue','pub = publish'],['gold','TAS'],['green','CAS'],['red','min = register min'],['purple','ret = return; first column is proposal']]);v.readout(()=>`Primitive ${primitive.value}; ${primitive.value==='register min'?'published-array candidate':'cell '+(A.cell??'sentinel')}. Decisions ${A.decisions.map((x,p)=>x===null?(A.pc[p]>=2?'INVALID unpublished value':'pending'):x).join(', ')}. Claimed consensus numbers: registers1, TAS2, FIFO2, CAS∞. ${primitive.value==='TAS'&&n.value>2?'The displayed extension is deliberately outside the valid two-process construction.':''}`);}
}
]};
